using Global

~ flashback_start

# Setting: Ten months ago. A finance analyst, Priya, has flagged a discrepancy in the export revenue accounts and requested a meeting.

Priya: I'm not trying to cause problems. I just need the account codes on these three transactions to make sense before I close the quarter.

Priya: They're routed through a shell entity I can't find any record of.

- Explain it away with something plausible. [if compliance >= 0]
	Jason: That's a logistics partner we use for a few overseas accounts. I can get you the paperwork.
	do compliance += 1
	=> priya_deflected

- Warn her off, carefully. [if leverage >= 0]
	Jason: Priya, I'd let this one go. Some accounts are compartmentalized for a reason, and asking too loudly tends to get noticed by the wrong people.
	do leverage += 1
	=> priya_warned

- Go over her head immediately.
	Jason: I'll talk to your director directly about this. No need for you to spend more time on it.
	do compliance -= 1
	do leverage += 1
	=> priya_escalated

~ priya_deflected

Priya: (skeptical, but tired) ...Fine. Send it over and I'll close it out.

Jason: (The paperwork he sent her was real. It just wasn't for this deal.)

do Global.priya_path = "deflected"

=> close_call_end

~ priya_warned

Priya: (a pause, reading between the lines) ...Understood.

Jason: (She closed the quarter without another word. She also stopped making eye contact with him in the hallway after that.)

do Global.priya_path = "warned"

=> close_call_end

~ priya_escalated

Priya: (visibly unsettled) Oh — okay. Sure.

Jason: (Her director called it "handled" within the hour. Jason never found out what that cost, or who paid it.)

do Global.priya_path = "escalated"
do Global.leverage -= 1
do Global.roll_escalation_gamble()

=> close_call_end

~ close_call_end

Jason: (It went away. That was the part that scared him — how easily it went away.)

do Global.return_from_flashback()

=> END
